                "text": "Let me understand the rule. Input has two intersecting lines of different colors. Find the intersection point. Surround it with a 3x3 yellow (4) border, leaving the center in its original color.\n\nThe verifier: mostcolor is the background (0). palette minus background gives two colors. For each color, take ofcolor -> backdrop (bounding box). The intersection of the two backdrops is... the overlapping region. Since lines are thin (1 wide), the backdrops are the full row band or column band. Actually for a vertical line, ofcolor gives cells all in one column; backdrop is the bounding box of those cells = the column from topmost to bottommost. For horizontal line, backdrop = the row. Intersection = the single crossing cell region... Actually intersection of column bbox and row bbox = single cell? Column bbox spans rows top to bottom at column j; row bbox spans columns left to right at row i. Intersection is (i,j) single point. Then outbox gives the 3x3 border around that point.\n\nSo output: at the intersection point, draw a 3x3 ring of 4 around it.\n\nNow note: the lines might not be full-length. In the example, vertical line color 6 at column 2, rows 0-4. Horizontal line color 9 at row 3, cols 0-4. Intersection at (3,2). outbox = ring around (3,2): rows 2-4, cols 1-3, all cells except center. So output has 4s at (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(3,1),(3,3),(4,1),(4,2),(4,3). Center (3,2)=6 kept.\n\nNow I need to make 3 demonstration pairs. They should vary and be valid. Let me just create simple ones with different colors, sizes, line positions.\n\nMust ensure the verifier's mostcolor assumption: background is most common color. So background must be the most common. Lines of two colors.\n\nImportant: the two line colors must be the non-background colors, and palette minus background must have exactly 2 colors. So input must have exactly 2 non-background colors.\n\nLet me design variety:\n\nPair 1: vertical line at col 1 (color 2), horizontal at row 2 (color 5), background 0. Grid 5x5.\n\nActually let me make grids where lines span full width/height so backdrops are full band. That's fine.\n\nLet me just take the example and vary.\n\nPair A (5x5, similar to example but different colors/positions):\nInput:\nrow0: 0,3,0,0,0\nrow1: 0,3,0,0,0\nrow2: 7,3,7,7,7\nrow3: 0,3,0,0,0\nrow4: 0,3,0,0,0\nVertical color 3 at col1 rows0-4, horizontal color 7 at row2 cols0-4. Intersection (2,1). Ring rows1-3, cols0-2. Output ring 4s.\n\nLet me verify with verifier logic: mostcolor 0 (count: total 25, 3 appears 5, 7 appears 5, so 0 count 15). palette {0,3,7}, remove 0 -> {3,7}. x4,x5 order arbitrary but symmetric. ofcolor(3) backdrop = rows0-4,col1. ofcolor(7) backdrop=row2,cols0-4. intersection = {(2,1)}. outbox = ring rows1-3 cols0-2 all except center. fill 4.\n\nOutput:\nrow0: 0,3,0,0,0\nrow1: 4,4,4,0,0\nrow2: 4,3,4,7,7\nrow3: 4,4,4,0,0\nrow4: 0,3,0,0,0\n\nHmm wait, row2 originally 7,3,7,7,7. ring at row2 cols0,2 -> set to 4. So row2: 4,3,4,7,7. Yes.\n\nPair B: different size, 4x4? Need lines spanning. vertical at col 3, horizontal at row 1, colors 8 and 1, background 0.\nInput:\nrow0: 0,0,0,8\nrow1: 1,1,1,8\nrow2: 0,0,0,8\nrow3: 0,0,0,8\nIntersection (1,3). Ring rows0-2, cols2-4 but col4 out of bounds. outbox: ai=0,aj=2,bi=2,bj=4. Cells: vlines (0,2),(1,2),(2,2),(0,4),(1,4),(2,4); hlines (0,2),(0,3),(0,4),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4). Union: (0,2),(1,2),(2,2),(0,4),(1,4),(2,4),(0,3),(2,3). Col4 out of bounds so fill skips. So effectively ring around (1,3) truncated: cells (0,2),(1,2),(2,2),(0,3),(2,3) and col4 skipped.\n\nHmm that makes a partial ring which might be confusing. Better to keep grid large enough that ring fully fits. Let me make lines positioned so intersection has room. But actually the rule says \"surround the middle point with a 3x3 yellow border\". For teaching, better keep full rings.\n\nLet me use a 5x5 or 6x6 with intersection interior.\n\nPair B: 6x6, background 0, vertical color 8 col2, horizontal color 1 row3.\nrows0-5